module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.gc
import sh.sysl.kairos.uv.Signals
import sh.sysl.libuv
import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}

// What a program is told when something outside it asks it to stop.
//
//     onSignal("SIGTERM", () ->
//         print("shutting down")
//         close(server))
//
// **This is how a server is stopped, and nothing else is.** A program with a listening socket in it
// runs until something closes that socket, so the only way it ever ends is from the inside -- and
// every way a deployment has of asking it to stop is a signal. A container stopping, a `systemd`
// unit restarting and Ctrl-C at a terminal are all `SIGTERM` or `SIGINT`, and a program that cannot
// hear one is a program that can only be killed: whatever it was in the middle of answering is
// dropped where it stood.
//
// **It is not a signal handler**, in the sense that word has in C. libuv catches the signal in
// whatever way the platform allows and delivers it to the loop, where a kairos task waiting on the
// watcher wakes in the same pass, so what runs here is an ordinary slate function at an ordinary
// time, between one turn of the loop and the next. Nothing about it is
// restricted to what is async-signal-safe: it may print, allocate, close a socket and start a timer,
// which is exactly what a program shutting down needs to do.
//
// **A handler is handed nothing, and the registration is what names the signal.** slate checks the
// count of a call's arguments, so a name passed to the handler would be a parameter every program
// had to write and nearly none would read -- node hands one over because one JavaScript function is
// commonly registered for several signals and JavaScript ignores what it is not expecting. A program
// here that wants one function for two signals registers it twice, and a program that needs to tell
// them apart says so at the two registrations rather than in a branch inside the handler.
//
// ## A watcher does not keep the program alive
//
// **This is node's rule and it is deliberate.** `node -e 'process.on("SIGINT", () => {})'` prints
// nothing and exits at once, and a slate program that registers a handler and does nothing else does
// the same. The alternative is worse than it looks: a script that installs a `SIGTERM` handler out
// of habit would never end, and the reason would be invisible in the source.
//
// So a watcher is put in kairos's **background** and its slot is **not** counted in `outstanding`.
// A background watcher is not counted by the loop even while its task waits in `next`, and the task
// is left parked rather than cancelled when nothing else is alive. What keeps a server
// running is the server -- the listening socket's slot, exactly as before -- and the handler is
// something that happens to be armed while it does.
//
// ## Why this is a table of its own rather than a slot in `event.sysl`'s
//
// `Pending` is what the loop is **waiting for**, and `outstanding` is how many of those there are:
// a slot in it is a reason for the program to still be running. A signal watcher is the opposite
// kind of thing -- something armed for as long as the program happens to last -- so putting one in
// that table would have meant claiming a slot and then undoing the count it exists to keep.
//
// Everything else about the arrangement is the same, and for the same reasons: the collector cannot
// see through an ARC box, so a callback captures a `usize` into this table rather than the slate
// closure it means to call, and the closure itself lives in the slot where the mark phase can reach
// it. A `&Signals` is a counted box and may not live in the collected heap at all, so it lives here
// too. The task that waits on it carries the slot, its generation and `run_stamp()` -- the
// arrangement `net.sysl` uses for a reader -- so a task woken after its handler was stopped, or
// after the program that armed it has ended, does nothing.

// One entry in the table: either a handler a program registered, or the watcher for one signal.
//
// ## One watcher per signal, and the handlers in registration order
//
// **libuv calls the watchers of one signal in the order of its own tree, which is by handle address,
// so a watcher per handler would run the handlers in the allocator's order.** node calls a signal's
// listeners in the order they were registered, and slate follows JavaScript where a choice is
// arbitrary, so there is ONE watcher per signal (a *trunk*) and one task on it. When a signal
// arrives that task calls the signal's handlers itself, in the order of their `gen` -- the stamp
// only ever goes up, so it is the order of registration however the slots were reused.
//
// **The list is taken when the signal arrives and every handler in it runs**, which is node's
// `emit`: a handler added by one of them is heard from the NEXT arrival on, and one stopped by an
// earlier handler still runs for this one. A handler that faults stops the program with its fault
// and the ones after it are not called, as an exception thrown by a listener is in node.
//
// Not `private`: `vm_state.sysl` names it in `Vm`.
struct Watch
    // Whether this slot is in use. A spent slot is reused rather than grown past.
    live: bool

    // The slate function to call. `Null` once the slot is spent, which is what makes it collectable
    // again -- the flag is what the mark phase reads, and a live-looking slot holding a dead closure
    // would keep it alive forever. A trunk has none.
    callback: Value

    // The watcher itself, kept alive here for as long as the program may still be sent the signal.
    // Only a trunk holds one; a handler is called by its signal's trunk.
    watcher: Option[&Signals]

    // The signal's number, which a handler names its trunk by and which `stop_watch` needs for the
    // one signal whose default is put back.
    number: i32

    // Which occupant of this slot this is, and it is what makes an id safe to hand a program. A
    // spent slot is reused, so an id on its own names a *position* rather than a thing: held across
    // an `offSignal` it would come to mean whatever took the position next, and stopping a handler
    // by a stale id would stop somebody else's. `event.sysl`'s `Pending.gen` is the same field for
    // the same reason. It is also what orders a signal's handlers.
    gen: usize

    // Whether this is a signal's watcher rather than a handler.
    trunk: bool

// Every signal this program is watching, and the generation stamp, are now `current().watches` and
// `current().watch_generation` -- see `vm_state.sysl`.

// Mark what a signal may still call.
//
// Not `private`: `collector.sysl` roots the collector's walk and this is one of the places it reaches.
mark_watches(h: *gc.Heap)
    val watches = current().watches

    for i in 0..<watches.len()
        if watches.at(i).live
            mark_value(h, watches.at(i).callback)

// Give the table back between runs.
//
// **Every watcher is stopped and not merely dropped, and that matters more here than it does for a
// timer.** A handle holds a reference to itself while it is open, so letting go of the last name for
// one stops nothing -- and a `SIGTERM` watcher left armed from a program that has finished would go
// on catching `SIGTERM` for the process it was running in. In the test suite that process is the
// suite, which would then quietly stop being stoppable.
//
// Not `private`: `event.sysl` puts the loop back to nothing between programs and this goes with it.
forget_watches()
    for i in 0..<current().watches.len()
        stop_watch(i)

    val v = current()

    v.watches.clear()
    v.watch_generation = 0

// Take a slot, reusing a spent one where there is one. `event.sysl`'s `claim` is the same shape.
private claim_watch(w: Watch) -> usize
    val v = current()
    var slot = w

    v.watch_generation += 1
    slot.gen = v.watch_generation

    for i in 0..<v.watches.len()
        if !v.watches.at(i).live
            v.watches.set(i, slot)
            return i

    v.watches.push(slot)
    v.watches.len() - 1
end claim_watch

// Stop watching, and give the slot back.
//
// **Closing is what stops it.** libuv takes a handle off the signal as it closes, and the task
// waiting in `next` ends with `ECANCELED` and returns, so there is no second call to make.
private stop_watch(id: usize)
    val v = current()

    if id >= v.watches.len() then return

    var w = v.watches.at(id)

    if !w.live then return

    w.live = false
    w.callback = Null

    w.watcher match
        Some(s) -> s.close()
        None -> ()

    w.watcher = None
    v.watches.set(id, w)

    // **The last handler of a signal takes its watcher with it**, so the signal goes back to what it
    // did before anybody asked.
    //
    // **Except `SIGUSR1` while the profiler is on**: that trunk is what dumps the profile, so a
    // program that stops its own handler hands the signal back to the dump and not to the default,
    // which would end the process.
    if !w.trunk && !watching(w.number) && !(w.number == libuv.SIGUSR1 && profiling())
        trunk_of(w.number) match
            Some(t) -> stop_watch(t)
            None -> ()

    // **libuv puts a signal back to its DEFAULT when the last watcher of it closes**, and `SIGPIPE`'s
    // default ends the process -- so a program that watched it and stopped would be killed by the
    // next write to a peer that had gone. It goes back to ignored instead, which is what it was before
    // anybody watched it; `event.sysl`'s `reset_events` says why that is the rule.
    if w.number == libuv.SIGPIPE && !watching(libuv.SIGPIPE) then libuv.ignore_sigpipe()
end stop_watch

// Whether any live handler is on this signal.
private watching(number: i32) -> bool
    val watches = current().watches

    for i in 0..<watches.len()
        if watches.at(i).live && !watches.at(i).trunk && watches.at(i).number == number then return true

    false

// The slot of the watcher this signal has, if it has one.
private trunk_of(number: i32) -> Option[usize]
    val watches = current().watches

    for i in 0..<watches.len()
        if watches.at(i).live && watches.at(i).trunk && watches.at(i).number == number then return Some(i)

    None

// What this signal's handlers are, in the order they were registered.
private handlers_in_order(number: i32) -> Buf[Value]
    val watches = current().watches
    var found: Buf[Value] = buf()
    var after: usize = 0

    loop
        var pick = watches.len()
        var least: usize = 0

        for i in 0..<watches.len()
            val w = watches.at(i)

            if w.live && !w.trunk && w.number == number && w.gen > after && (pick == watches.len() || w.gen < least)
                pick = i
                least = w.gen

        if pick == watches.len() then break

        found.push(watches.at(pick).callback)
        after = least

    found
end handlers_in_order

// Call every handler a signal has, in the order they were registered.
//
// **A fault inside a handler stops the program**, as one inside a timer callback does and as an
// uncaught throw does in node, and the handlers after it are not called -- node's `emit` lets the
// exception leave the loop over the listeners. There is no statement to attach it to -- the loop is
// not running any -- so it is reported against the whole run.
//
// **Every function is held before the first runs.** The list is a sysl local and so no root, and a
// handler may stop a later one (which empties its slot) or allocate enough to collect.
private fire_signal(number: i32)
    val calls = handlers_in_order(number)

    // **A program's own `SIGUSR1` handler takes the signal and the profile is not dumped**, because a
    // program that registered one has said what the signal means to it. The dump is what the signal
    // means only while nobody has claimed it; `SLATE_PROFILE`'s own note says so.
    if calls.len() == 0
        if number == libuv.SIGUSR1 && profiling() then profile_dump()

        return

    val mark = root_mark()
    var faulted = false

    for i in 0..<calls.len()
        hold(calls.at(i))

    for i in 0..<calls.len()
        if faulted then break

        var none: Buf[Value] = buf()

        apply_callback(calls.at(i), none, point(0), "onSignal") match
            Ok(_) -> ()
            Err(s) ->
                current().loop_fault = Some(s)
                faulted = true

    release(mark)
end fire_signal

// The number a name stands for, which is the platform's and is asked for rather than written down.
//
// **`SIGKILL` and `SIGSTOP` are named here even though neither can be caught**, so that a program
// asking for one is told what is true of it rather than that no such signal exists. A program that
// named `SIGKILL` meant it, and the answer it needs is that nothing of its own will run.
// Not `private`: `spawn.sysl` names a signal the same way, `child.kill("SIGTERM")` being the other
// place a program writes one.
signal_number(name: string) -> Option[i32]
    if name == "SIGHUP" then return Some(libuv.SIGHUP)
    if name == "SIGINT" then return Some(libuv.SIGINT)
    if name == "SIGQUIT" then return Some(libuv.SIGQUIT)
    if name == "SIGABRT" then return Some(libuv.SIGABRT)
    if name == "SIGKILL" then return Some(libuv.SIGKILL)
    if name == "SIGPIPE" then return Some(libuv.SIGPIPE)
    if name == "SIGALRM" then return Some(libuv.SIGALRM)
    if name == "SIGTERM" then return Some(libuv.SIGTERM)
    if name == "SIGCHLD" then return Some(libuv.SIGCHLD)
    if name == "SIGCONT" then return Some(libuv.SIGCONT)
    if name == "SIGSTOP" then return Some(libuv.SIGSTOP)
    if name == "SIGTSTP" then return Some(libuv.SIGTSTP)
    if name == "SIGUSR1" then return Some(libuv.SIGUSR1)
    if name == "SIGUSR2" then return Some(libuv.SIGUSR2)
    if name == "SIGWINCH" then return Some(libuv.SIGWINCH)

    None
end signal_number

// What a name nobody knows is told about instead. Every catchable one, in the order above.
private val Catchable: string =
    "SIGHUP, SIGINT, SIGQUIT, SIGABRT, SIGPIPE, SIGALRM, SIGTERM, SIGCHLD, SIGCONT, SIGTSTP, " +
    "SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2 and SIGWINCH"

// The same list where the signal is being SENT rather than caught, which is `child.kill`'s case:
// `SIGKILL` and `SIGSTOP` belong here, a program being perfectly able to send either.
//
// Not `private`: `spawn.sysl` refuses a name it does not know in these words.
signal_choices() -> string =
    s"the ones a program may send are ${Catchable}, SIGKILL and SIGSTOP"

// -- what a program calls --------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `onSignal(name, fn)` -- call `fn` every time this program is sent that signal.
//
// The answer is an id, which `offSignal` takes and nothing else reads. That it is a number at all is
// `setTimeout`'s convention, and it carries a generation for that function's reason.
do_on_signal(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    wants(args, 2, at, "onSignal", "a signal name and a function")?

    val name = text_argument(args.at(0), at, "onSignal", "a signal name")?
    val cb = args.at(1)

    cb match
        Fn(_) -> ()
        Native(_) -> ()
        HostFn(_) -> ()
        other -> return fault(at, s"`onSignal` needs a function to call, and this is ${other.type_name()}")

    // **Refused by name, before libuv is asked.** `start` on either answers `EINVAL`, which is a
    // sentence about an invalid argument where the truth is about the kernel.
    if name == "SIGKILL" || name == "SIGSTOP"
        return fault(at, s"`${name}` cannot be caught: the kernel acts on it itself, and nothing this program says will run first")

    signal_number(name) match
        None -> fault(at, s"there is no signal called `${name}`; the ones a program may catch are ${Catchable}")
        Some(number) -> watch_signal(number, cb, at)
end do_on_signal

// Register a handler, arming the signal's watcher if this is the first handler it has.
private watch_signal(number: i32, cb: Value, at: Span) -> Step
    if trunk_of(number).is_none()
        current().io.signals(number) match
            Err(e) -> return fault(at, s"this signal could not be watched: ${e}")
            Ok(s) -> arm_trunk(s, number)

    val id = claim_watch(Watch(true, cb, None, number, 0, false))

    Ok(Int(long(current().watches.at(id).gen)))
end watch_signal

// Put a signal's one watcher in the table and start the task that waits on it.
private arm_trunk(s: &Signals, number: i32)
    // **Armed, and not a reason to keep running.** See the header: a program with nothing
    // but a handler in it ends, exactly as node's does.
    s.background()

    val id = claim_watch(Watch(true, Null, Some(s), number, 0, true))
    val gen = current().watches.at(id).gen

    // **The task carries the id and not the value**, which is the arrangement the collector
    // needs: it cannot see through a task, so what the task holds must be something it does
    // not have to see through.
    loop_events().spawn(signal_arrivals(s, id, gen, number, run_stamp()))
end arm_trunk

// Arm `SIGUSR1` so that a profiled program dumps its report when it is sent one.
//
// **It is the signal's ordinary trunk with no handlers on it**, so it is a watcher that does not keep
// the program alive (`arm_trunk` backgrounds it) and that a program's own `onSignal("SIGUSR1", ...)`
// finds already there and shares. `fire_signal` is what decides who answers: handlers, if there are
// any, and the profile dump if there are none. Not `private`: `run.sysl` calls it where a profiled
// program starts, after `reset_events` has put the table back -- armed earlier it would be closed.
watch_profile_dump()
    if trunk_of(libuv.SIGUSR1).is_some() then return

    current().io.signals(libuv.SIGUSR1) match
        Err(_) -> ()
        Ok(s) -> arm_trunk(s, libuv.SIGUSR1)
end watch_profile_dump

// The task `arm_trunk` spawns: one round of handler calls per arrival, for as long as the slot is this one.
//
// **It ends three ways and each is a `return`**: the watcher closed under it (the last `offSignal`,
// or a reset's `forget_watches`), which `next` answers with `ECANCELED`; the program that armed it
// having been given back, which the stamp says; and the slot having been spent and taken by somebody
// else, which the generation says.
private async signal_arrivals(s: &Signals, id: usize, gen: usize, number: i32, stamp: usize)
    loop
        val got = await s.next()

        if !same_run(stamp) then return

        got match
            Err(_) -> return
            Ok(_) -> ()

        val watches = current().watches

        if id >= watches.len() || !watches.at(id).live || watches.at(id).gen != gen then return

        fire_signal(number)
end signal_arrivals

// `offSignal(id)` -- stop the handler that id names, and go back to whatever the signal did before.
//
// **An id that names nothing is not an error**, which is `clearTimeout`'s rule: a program that stops
// a handler twice, or stops one after the run that registered it, has said something true about what
// it wants and there is nothing to report.
do_off_signal(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    wants(args, 1, at, "offSignal", "the id `onSignal` answered")?

    args.at(0) match
        Int(n) ->
            if n >= 0
                val watches = current().watches

                for i in 0..<watches.len()
                    if watches.at(i).live && watches.at(i).gen == usize(n)
                        stop_watch(i)
                        break

            Ok(Null)
        other -> fault(at, s"a signal handler is stopped by its id, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_off_signal

// -- what this file registers ---------------------------------------------------------------------

// Being asked to stop by something outside the program, which is the other half of `exit` and the
// only way a server ever ends. In `slate:process` for the same reason `exit` is: what this process is
// told and what it tells the shell are one subject.
var n_on_signal: NativeFn = 0
var n_off_signal: NativeFn = 0

install_signals(env: *EnvObj, everything: bool)
    n_on_signal = register("onSignal", do_on_signal)
    n_off_signal = register("offSignal", do_off_signal)

    if everything
        declare(env, "onSignal", Native(n_on_signal), false)
        declare(env, "offSignal", Native(n_off_signal), false)
end install_signals
